body:
The error should show in the terminal, or in the details in the debugger, which line of code it originates from.
It may be one of those three:
```
core/variant/array.cpp
855:    ERR_FAIL_COND_MSG(script.is_valid() && p_class_name == StringName(), "Script class can only be set together with base class name");

core/variant/dictionary.cpp
609:    ERR_FAIL_COND_MSG(key_script.is_valid() && p_key_class_name == StringName(), "Script class can only be set together with base class name.");
611:    ERR_FAIL_COND_MSG(value_script.is_valid() && p_value_class_name == StringName(), "Script class can only be set together with base class name.");
```

So it seems related to typed arrays and dictionaries. The error could likely be made more explicit and maybe provide information about the script. Here it looks like you're using a typed array or dictionary with a script class that doesn't use `class_name`?

I believe I've found what's causing the issue for me at least, though I don't understand why. I have two C# scripts, the `InFlowActionMap.cs` which looks like this:

```cs
namespace RobProductions.GDInFlow
{
	[GlobalClass]
	public partial class InFlowActionMap : Resource
	{
		[Export]
		public Array<string> actions = new Array<string>();
	}
}
```

And the `InFlowSetupAsset.cs` which looks like this:

```cs
namespace RobProductions.GDInFlow
{
	[GlobalClass]
	public partial class InFlowSetupAsset : Resource
	{
		[Export]
		public Array<InFlowActionMap> actionMaps = new Array<InFlowActionMap>();
	}
}
```

And compiling that gives me the "script class can only be set together" error, so for me it doesn't have anything to do with classes being in unique scripts. However when I remove the `= new Array<InFlowActionMap>()` part, the error goes away. It seems like it's throwing a problem when you try to initialize the Godot Array explicitly in the same line as the definition. Would be nice to know why that's the case and if it's possible to fix.

However, there's also a secondary (maybe unrelated) problem in that neither of these Resources show up in the "create resource" window. I tried making a test script that looks like this:

```cs
[GlobalClass]
public partial class TestResource : Resource
{
	[Export]
	public Array<bool> hi;
}
```

And it worked just fine, so I'm assuming the namespace is to blame. Is that really a limitation of the C# implementation, that namespaced Resources can't become [GlobalClass]? I might be missing something obvious but I don't think I've read anything about that before...

**Edit:** Turns out that secondary issue had nothing to do with namespaces but the location of the file. Since it was in an addon folder that also had a `plugin.cfg` file, all of the code was being treated as a plugin. One weird quirk of plugins that I didn't expect was that Resources defined in plugins don't show up in the Resource picker unless you "enable" the plugin from the project settings. So that solved the problem. Hope that was helpful in case anyone has the same problem :)
